  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I didn't open the dashboard to check how the panels render, and I didn't confirm the rule's 5xx query matches any documents.\n\n**Data:** The logs data view is time-based on `timestamp`. The fields I used are `response` (a string with a `.keyword` subfield), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `url` and `host`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-33709373 Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Responses by status code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by extension, as a bar chart.\n\nIt's at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-33709373-web-logs-overview-2363b89ba990`.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-33709373 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5xx`, over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. The rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it only creates an alert and doesn't notify anyone.",
